ime range. The user might need to check the last, say, 10 minutes of data to see recent dependencies. \n\nAlternatively, checking metrics with get_metrics could help. For example, looking at error rates, latency, or throughput for each service. But without knowing which service is faulty, it\'s hard to decide which metric to check. \n\nAnother approach is to look at traces using get_traces for each service. If a service has a high number of errors or long latency in its traces, that could indicate a problem. But again, without knowing which service is faulty, this might take a lot of time. \n\nWait, maybe the dependency graph can highlight services that are not interacting as expected. For example, if a service isn\'t showing up in the graph or has broken links, that could be a problem. So using get_dependency_graph with a reasonable time frame (like 10 minutes) might help visualize any issues. \n\nAlternatively, using get_operations to see what operations each service has. But the user might not have that information yet. \n\nAnother thought: the user could check the pods and deployments to see if any are in a bad state. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool can be used with "kubectl get pods" and "kubectl get deployments" in the astronomy-shop namespace. That would show the current state of the workloads. If any pods are crashing or deployments are not stable, that\'s a starting point. \n\nBut the user hasn\'t checked the actual pod statuses yet. So maybe the next step is to use kubectl to get the pods and deployments. Let me check the available tools again. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can run "kubectl get pods" and "kubectl get deployments" in the specified namespace. \n\nSo, first, check the pods to see if any are in CrashLoopBackOff, Error, or similar states. Then check deployments to see their replicas and availability. That would give a baseline of the current state of the services. \n\nBut the user might not have access to the namespace, so the command should include the namespace. For example, "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop" and "kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop". \n\nSo the next tool to use would be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop" to check the pod statuses. Then, after that, check deployments. \n\nAlternatively, maybe start with the dependency graph to see if there\'s a service that\'s not connecting properly. But without knowing which service is faulty, checking the pods first might be more straightforward. \n\nHmm. The user\'s initial step was to get the list of services. Now, the next logical step is to check the current state of the pods and deployments to see if any are in a problematic state. So using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get pods and deployments would be the next step. \n\nTherefore, the tool choice would be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop" to check the pod statuses. That would help identify if any pods are not running or have errors.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nTo assess the current state of the application, I need to check the status of pods and deployments in the `astronomy-shop` namespace. This will reveal if any services are not running, have crashes, or are in an unstable state. Using `kubectl get pods` will show pod statuses, while `kubectl get deployments` will confirm replica set health and availability. This is critical for identifying immediate issues in the workload state. \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 980, 'prompt_tokens': 1728, 'total_tokens': 2708, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb5f-3e3f-7f50-a1ef-14acfef20e10-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1728, 'output_tokens': 980, 'total_tokens': 2708}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='6e657a2f-4cde-4631-9f49-1446feafec9d'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an application with many microservices. They\'ve already used get_services to list the services and then used kubectl get pods to check the pod statuses. The next step is to check the deployments to see if there are any issues with replicas or rollout statuses. So, I should call exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd again with the command "kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop". That will help identify if any deployments are failing or have scaling issues. If there are problems with deployments, that could be the root cause of the faults in the services. Let me make sure the command is correct and in the right namespace.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8e9aea45186c1a26', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 169, 'prompt_tokens': 2755, 'total_tokens': 2924, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb5f-6ab8-78c0-bc71-802b881b0796-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8e9aea45186c1a26', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2755, 'output_tokens': 169, 'total_tokens': 2924}), T